Jasmin ransomware employs a multi-stage unpacking process using a custom LZMA-based packer with entropy obfuscation. The loader (`loader.dll`) checks virtual memory size, execution time, and user activity via `GetSystemTimes()` to detect sandboxes. If conditions suggest a sandbox, it sleeps for 30 minutes before proceeding. For persistence, it abuses Windows Registry "RunOnce" keys with a delayed execution trick:

```powershell
reg add "HKCU\Software\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\RunOnce" /v "Update" /t REG_SZ /d "cmd /c start /min powershell -windowstyle hidden -exec bypass -file C:\Users\Public\update.ps1"
```

For C2, Jasmin queries Microsoft Graph API (`graph.microsoft.com`) using hardcoded OAuth tokens embedded in the binary. It exfiltrates stolen credentials via encrypted HTTPS POST to `https://api.onedrive.com/v1.0/drive/root:/Documents/.credentials.bin:/content`. Analysts can extract tokens using Frida hooks:

```javascript
Interceptor.attach(Module.findExportByName("ntdll.dll", "NtQueryValueKey"), {
    onEnter: function(args) {
        console.log("Registry key accessed: " + args[1].readUtf16String());
    }
});
```

Mitigation includes behavioral monitoring for unusual Graph API calls and registry key modifications. Group-IB observed Jasmin variants using this technique in attacks against EU finance sectors (2024-2025).

